{
  "requirement": "Each language's fingerprint rules determine which attachments\ncontribute to the owning item's structural hash.",
  "determination": true,
  "rationale": "**Obligation.** `requirements.sdk.outlines.attachments.fingerprint` has one clause: each language's fingerprint rules decide which attachments feed the owning item's structural hash. This breaks into two checks:\n- Attachments the language's rules select must change the owner's hash.\n- Attachments the rules exclude must not change it.\n\nI read the per-language `Profile` statics under `outline/languages/`. Only Rust (`hashes_comment: is_doc_comment`) and the decorators handled by the Python `attaches` rule contribute. CSS, Markdown, TOML, TypeScript, YAML and plain text hash no comments. The shared implementation is `outline/mod.rs#impl[Builder<'tree, 'source>]/fn[encode]`.\n\n**Test coverage.**\n- **Rust, doc comments included.** `outline/tests.rs#fn[hashes_skip_whitespace_and_plain_comments_but_not_doc_comments]` makes four checks:\n  - Changing the outer doc text (`Doc.` to `Docs.`) changes the hash.\n  - Changing the attribute (`#[inline]` to `#[cold]`) changes the hash.\n  - Changing a plain `//` comment, whitespace, or the line position keeps the hash.\n  - A parent `mod`'s structural hash changes when a child changes, while its identity hash does not.\n- **Rust, doc forms.** `outline/tests.rs#fn[reflowing_inner_and_block_doc_comments_keeps_the_hash]` covers inner `//!` and `/*! */` doc comments and `/** */` doc comments. Re-flowing the prose keeps the hash. Changing the words changes it.\n- **Python decorators.** `outline/tests.rs#fn[python_decorators_attach_to_the_decorated_definition_for_hashing]` makes four checks:\n  - Changing a decorator changes the hash.\n  - Removing a decorator changes the hash.\n  - Blank-line and indent changes keep the hash.\n  - A method's decorator changes the owning class's structural hash but not its identity hash.\n- **All bundled languages.** `tests/outline_content.rs#fn[bundled_fingerprints_exclude_comments_and_preserve_word_boundaries]` runs Rust, Python, TS, TSX, CSS, Markdown, TOML and YAML. For each, it checks that edited comments and whitespace keep the 64-hex-digit fingerprint and that joined words change it. This confirms the non-contributing attachments (TS JSDoc, CSS, TOML and YAML comments, Markdown HTML comments) don't affect the owner's hash. It also checks that a Rust `///` doc comment is not ignored.\n\n**Gaps.** I found none. Both directions of the obligation, contributing and non-contributing, are asserted for the languages with attachments and for every bundled language without them. Exact doc-comment classification edge cases such as `////` belong to the per-language `...fingerprints` requirements, which have their own tests. I did not treat them as gaps here. No `{fixme}` acknowledgments apply.",
  "references": "1c74bf63aa9a7132fdf54df37896eed61aea591d0b7ac84ebf8acf5682020bc0",
